            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nBefore you continue, review the two previous texts: **equality** and **natural limits**. This lesson builds on both",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "82544083-aaad-4760-963b-f433bd3e8729",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Are we truly equal?",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "18a901e4-3da3-45d8-a239-fb551e78698e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Natural limits",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nIn the previous texts we introduced the idea of equality: two objects are either equal or they are not. If they are not equal, they are different. But the word \"different\" alone says nothing about *how much* they differ. To measure that gap we need a way to compare one object with another. That is the goal of this lesson: you will learn to say which of two numbers is **greater** and which is **smaller**, and how these two ideas help us define limits.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_numbers",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<h3>Numerical Relationship</h3><p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgMeter\"  class=\"imgcenter\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgMeter', '0%')\" /><b>Figure 1</b>: Representation of a centesimal scale.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Look at Figure 1: the numbers we studied earlier are listed in order. When a number comes **after** another, it is **greater**. We write that relationship with the symbol (>), so *21 > 20* means \"21 is greater than 20\"",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c47a586f-6053-4b8e-8f9b-828cd6220110",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "The opposite is also true: a number that comes **before** another is **smaller**. We use the symbol (<), so *20 < 21* means \"20 is smaller than 21\"",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4e3c3945-0bf4-4040-9735-546541b27a50",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "A useful tip: the open side of the comparison symbol — greater (>) or smaller (<) — always faces the larger number. Test it with *21 > 20*: the opening faces 21.\n\nWhen the gap between the two numbers is huge, mathematics offers a shortcut: (&#8811;) reads \"much greater than\", as in *1000000 &#8811; 20*, and (&#8810;) reads \"much less than\", as in *20 &#8810; 1000000*",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c47a586f-6053-4b8e-8f9b-828cd6220110",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4e3c3945-0bf4-4040-9735-546541b27a50",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><h4>Practice</h4>An inequality is a statement that must stay true. If you change one side, you have to change the other side in the same proportion, or the comparison breaks. Try it yourself: use the arrows to move the hands up and down, and watch how both numbers change together:</p><p><table id=\"yupana0\" class=\"tawapukllay\"><tr><th colspan=\"4\"><i>Equation</i></th><th>Left hand</th><th>Right hand</th><th>Arrows</th></tr> <tr id=\"tf1\"><td id=\"tc1f1\"><span class=\"text_to_paint\" id=\"leftnum\">0</span></td> <td id=\"tc2f1\" colspan=\"2\"><span class=\"text_to_paint\">&lt;</span></td> <td id=\"tc4f1\"><span class=\"text_to_paint\" id=\"rightnum\">1</span></td><td id=\"tc5f1\" style=\"background-color:white;\"><img id=\"leftHandImg\"  /></td><td id=\"tc6f1\"><img id=\"rightHandImg\"  /></td><td id=\"tc7f1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-caret-up upArrowWithFA\" id=\"traineeUp0\"></i>  <i class=\"fa-solid fa-caret-down downArrowWithFA\" style=\"font-size:4em;color:#00f;\" id=\"traineeDown0\"></i></td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Keeping the inequality.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_bulding",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Smaller as a Basis\n\nNature always starts small. First came the elementary particles, such as electrons. Those particles grouped into **atoms**, atoms joined into **molecules**, and molecules combined into ever more complex structures. Out of that long chain came the first living beings, and after billions of years of evolution, the great diversity of species we know today — including ourselves.\n\nNotice the pattern: each new level is built from the one below it. We will use the same idea with numbers",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6dfe4b3b-8338-46ab-8149-2e073414907e",
                     "text": "NASA",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "34a775e9-d5b7-4bed-b5cd-28f77fc1aa65",
                     "text": "Moody, E.R.R., Álvarez-Carretero, S., Mahendrarajah, T.A. et al",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "27ff155c-c38d-4619-a435-4d95fd55071d",
                     "text": "Pirula",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "822840ab-a8dd-4f6a-b53e-ca5ab8d159ca",
                     "text": "Pirula",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d7dd7faf-23a6-4cb4-9c1a-a9bbb7d6f33d",
                     "text": "Hossenfelder, Sabine",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table><tr><th>Quantum atom</th><th>DNA molecule</th><th>Living Beings</th></tr><tr><td><img id=\"imgQA\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgQA', '0%')\"  /></td><td><img id=\"imgDNA\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgDNA', '0%')\"  style=\"width:90%\" /></td><td><img id=\"img2Cat\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('img2Cat', '0%')\" style=\"width:70%;\"></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align: center; font-size: 70%;\"><b>Figure 2</b>: Simplified representation of a hydrogen atom on the left, a DNA molecule in the center, and living beings on the right.</td></tr></table></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Superposition\n\nHumans build in the same way: we stack smaller parts to obtain a larger whole. **Temple 16 of Copán** (Honduras) is a good example. Over about 400 years it went through several construction phases. As Figure 3 shows, builders raised a small structure first and then added layer on layer of new material, until the temple reached the size we see today",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "daae87a2-5fa9-43d1-88fb-6713b25d50d5",
                     "text": "Tuarez Mejia, L. F.",
                     "page": "pp. 120-128",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2023",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgCopanTemple\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgCopanTemple', '0%')\"  class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 3</b>: Poster at the archaeological site of Copán (<htcite0>). Photo taken on <htdate1>, during a visit to the site.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1e007481-9bd3-4a09-ad4d-6e812fe79641",
                     "text": "Instituto Hondureño de Antropologia e Historia",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "02",
                        "day": "01"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2024",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2015",
                     "month": "7",
                     "day": "30"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_example",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Beginning Small\n\nThe same logic appears in the great monuments of the Americas (<i>Abya Yala</i>). The pyramids of **Figure 4** have wide bases that narrow as they rise, much like the mountains near these archaeological sites, which probably inspired their shape.\n\nThe landscape was not the only guide: recent discoveries show that the sacred city of **Caral** (Peru) was planned with small scale models — maquettes — before its great structures were raised",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "720f6dfd-4b86-4e07-a887-1010e1041a4d",
                     "text": "Ruta Caral",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "03c3743c-d268-4730-ba8e-d36b5c49e781",
                     "text": "Caral Perú Oficial",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "3b18a263-71e9-4c61-a7fe-c23cbce65624",
                     "text": "Zona Caral",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2023",
                        "month": "9",
                        "day": "28"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b9af756b-558a-4292-b71b-d9b3cc47d570",
                     "text": "de la Morena, Raquel",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgCaralPiramideH1\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgCaralPiramideH1', '0%')\"  class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 4</b>: Pyramid H1 in the archaeological site of Caral (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>). Photo taken on <htdate2>, during a visit to the site.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "720f6dfd-4b86-4e07-a887-1010e1041a4d",
                     "text": "Ruta Caral",
