      {
         "id": "SECTION_limits",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Limits\n\n\"Greater\" and \"smaller\" are not only ways to compare two numbers: they are also the language of **limits**. In the previous text we saw that a limit can be a maximum or a minimum, and that it can also be read as an approximation of a value. Now we will use the symbols we just learned to write those limits down",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "18a901e4-3da3-45d8-a239-fb551e78698e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Natural limits",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg fill=\"none\" stroke=\"black\"><circle cx=\"135\" cy=\"75\" r=\"70\"></circle> <line x1=\"140\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"205\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"black\" /> <text x=\"135\" y=\"85\">O</text>  <text x=\"175\" y=\"85\">r</text> </svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 5</b>: Circle with a radius of 75: O is the center and r the radius.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Upper Limit\n\nAn upper limit is the largest value allowed in a given context. In **Figure 5**, the radius r (the distance from the center O to the circumference) measures 75. Nothing inside the circle can exceed that distance, so any radius we measure must stay below it: *r < 75*",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "e58ad730-899f-49b6-aaf8-53cb39c31f0e",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Lower Limit\n\nA lower limit is the smallest value allowed. Back to **Figure 5**: measure the radius from the circumference toward the center, and the segment only exists while it has some length. A radius of zero would be no radius at all, so the radius must stay above zero: *r > 0*",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "61b6b478-d49a-40ec-a68d-52767ebbb6cc",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Biological Limits\n\nLimits also matter in biology. When we try to decide whether two populations are genetically distinct, we compare them inside a range. Alan R. Templeton proposed a clear criterion: to avoid calling two populations different races, their genetic difference (*d*) must stay between a minimum of 0 and a maximum of 25: *0 < d < 25*",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "723b0735-b8c4-4b02-8ce6-6da033ca2454",
                     "text": "Templeton A. R.",
                     "page": "pp. 7 - 8",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "723b0735-b8c4-4b02-8ce6-6da033ca2454",
                     "text": "Templeton A. R.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "Templeton also pointed out that the genetic variation among humans stays below *5*, well inside that range (*0 < 5 < 25*)",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "723b0735-b8c4-4b02-8ce6-6da033ca2454",
                     "text": "Templeton A. R.",
                     "page": "pp. 7 - 8",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "723b0735-b8c4-4b02-8ce6-6da033ca2454",
                     "text": "Templeton A. R.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this lesson you learned to compare two numbers with the symbols (>) and (<), and to turn those comparisons into inequalities. You also saw that the same ideas describe limits — in geometry and even in biology — which gives a deeper meaning to the limits discussed in the previous chapter",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "18a901e4-3da3-45d8-a239-fb551e78698e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Natural limits",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
